<title>GERMANY: Waigel says euro will eliminate speculation.</title>
<headline>Waigel says euro will eliminate speculation.</headline>
<dateline>MUNICH, Germany 1997-03-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Finance Minister Theo Waigel said on Friday the planned single European currency, the euro, was indispensable, and would eliminate speculative pressure on Germany's currency.</p>
<p>The euro is in Germany's best interest, he said, according to the text of a speech prepared for delivery at a technology conference.</p>
<p>European monetary union (EMU) will create a currency region that is on an even footing with the dollar and yen, &quot;eliminating worldwide speculative pressure on our currency,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>Waigel cited a study by a Bavarian bank that said the single currency would add 70 billion marks to the German economy in its first decade.</p>
<p>EMU, scheduled to start in January 1999, faces strong popular opposition from ordinary Germans, who are reluctant to give up the mark, a symbol of postwar economic prosperity.</p>
<p>-- Terence Gallagher, Bonn newsroom, 49 228 2609750</p>
